<section class="section reveal" id="matrix-operations">
  <span class="section-label">Core Operations</span>
  <h2>Essential Matrix Operations</h2>

  <p>NumPy provides optimized implementations of fundamental linear algebra operations. These operations are vectorized in C and execute far faster than Python loops. Understanding which operation to use is critical for both correctness and performance.</p>

  <h3>Matrix Multiplication (Dot Product)</h3>

  <p>Matrix multiplication combines two arrays in a mathematically meaningful way. The dot product of two arrays depends on their shapes and dimensionality. This is different from element-wise multiplication and has strict dimensional requirements.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Matrix multiplication operations</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Create matrices</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>], [<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>]])
<span class="va">B</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>], [<span class="nm">7</span>, <span class="nm">8</span>]])

<span class="cm"># Method 1: np.dot() - most common for 2D arrays</span>
<span class="va">result1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">dot</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">B</span>)

<span class="cm"># Method 2: @ operator - modern Python style</span>
<span class="va">result2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">B</span>

<span class="cm"># Method 3: np.matmul() - general purpose</span>
<span class="va">result3</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">matmul</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">B</span>)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"All three methods produce identical results"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">result1</span>)

<span class="cm"># Element-wise multiplication is different!</span>
<span class="va">element_wise</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="va">B</span>  <span class="cm"># NOT matrix multiplication</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Element-wise (different from matrix mult):"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">element_wise</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening:</strong> We demonstrate three equivalent ways to perform matrix multiplication. The @ operator is preferred in modern NumPy code. Crucially, we show that * performs element-wise multiplication, not matrix multiplication. This distinction causes many bugs in AI code.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Transpose and Shape Manipulation</h3>

  <p>Transposition flips a matrix across its diagonal, swapping rows and columns. This operation is ubiquitous in machine learning—every time you need to match dimensions for multiplication, transposition helps.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Transpose operations</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], [<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]])
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Original shape: {A.shape}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># (2, 3)</span>

<span class="cm"># Transpose</span>
<span class="va">A_T</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span>.<span class="fn">T</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Transposed shape: {A_T.shape}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># (3, 2)</span>

<span class="cm"># Practical example: making dimensions compatible</span>
<span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>], [<span class="nm">2</span>], [<span class="nm">3</span>]])  <span class="cm"># Shape (3, 1)</span>

<span class="cm"># Can't multiply (2,3) @ (3,1) without transpose... wait, we can!</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">v</span>  <span class="cm"># (2,3) @ (3,1) = (2,1) ✓</span>

<span class="cm"># But this doesn't work</span>
<span class="cm"># bad = A @ A  # (2,3) @ (2,3) - inner dimensions don't match!</span>
<span class="cm"># Use transpose to fix it</span>
<span class="va">good</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">A</span>.<span class="fn">T</span>  <span class="cm"># (2,3) @ (3,2) = (2,2) ✓</span></code></pre>

  <h3>Matrix Inversion and Solving Linear Systems</h3>

  <p>Matrix inversion is useful but often not the best way to solve problems. Understanding when inversion is appropriate and when other methods are better is crucial for numerical stability and performance.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Matrix operations for solving Ax = b</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>], [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>]])
<span class="va">b</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">9</span>], [<span class="nm">8</span>]])

<span class="cm"># Method 1: Using inversion (less stable)</span>
<span class="va">A_inv</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">inv</span>(<span class="va">A</span>)
<span class="va">x_via_inv</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A_inv</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">b</span>

<span class="cm"># Method 2: Using solve (better for stability)</span>
<span class="va">x_via_solve</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">solve</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">b</span>)

<span class="cm"># Method 3: Using least squares (best for overdetermined systems)</span>
<span class="va">x_via_lstsq</span>, <span class="fn">residuals</span>, <span class="fn">rank</span>, <span class="fn">s</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">lstsq</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">b</span>, <span class="va">rcond</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">None</span>)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"All three methods give similar results, but solve is more stable"</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Deep Learning:</strong> Neural networks perform millions of matrix multiplications during training. Small numerical instabilities compound, leading to NaN losses or divergence. Using np.linalg.solve instead of matrix inversion prevents accumulation of rounding errors.</p>
    <p><strong>In Data Science:</strong> When fitting linear models or solving least-squares problems, numerical stability directly affects model quality. Using lstsq instead of inversion with small, nearly-singular matrices gives more stable results.</p>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Numerical precision is critical when dealing with large matrices representing portfolios. Inversion can amplify errors in nearly-singular matrices. Always prefer solve to inv.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Use @ or np.matmul for matrix multiplication, not *</li>
      <li>Transposition swaps rows and columns, useful for dimension matching</li>
      <li>Prefer np.linalg.solve to np.linalg.inv for solving linear systems</li>
      <li>Use lstsq for overdetermined systems with least-squares solutions</li>
      <li>Always check shapes before operations using .shape attribute</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

