<section class="section reveal" id="advanced-operations">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Concepts</span>
  <h2>Eigenvalues, Norms, and Advanced Operations</h2>

  <p>Beyond basic operations, linear algebra offers powerful tools for understanding matrix properties. Eigenvalues reveal inherent characteristics of transformations. Norms measure magnitude. These concepts appear throughout machine learning: in regularization, in dimensionality reduction, in convergence analysis.</p>

  <h3>Norms and Vector Magnitude</h3>

  <p>Norms measure the "size" of a vector. Different norms measure size differently. The choice of norm affects optimization, regularization, and distance metrics.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Computing norms</span>
<span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>])

<span class="cm"># L2 norm (Euclidean distance) - most common</span>
<span class="va">l2_norm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">norm</span>(<span class="va">v</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>)  <span class="cm"># sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5</span>

<span class="cm"># L1 norm (Manhattan distance)</span>
<span class="va">l1_norm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">norm</span>(<span class="va">v</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>)  <span class="cm"># |3| + |4| = 7</span>

<span class="cm"># Max norm (maximum absolute value)</span>
<span class="va">linf_norm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">norm</span>(<span class="va">v</span>, <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">inf</span>)  <span class="cm"># max(|3|, |4|) = 4</span>

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"L2 norm: {l2_norm}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"L1 norm: {l1_norm}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Max norm: {linf_norm}"</span>)</code></pre>

  <h3>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</h3>

  <p>Eigenvalues and eigenvectors reveal fundamental properties of matrices. They show directions that the matrix doesn't rotate, and how much it scales those directions. Understanding eigenvalues is crucial for many machine learning algorithms.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Computing eigenvalues and eigenvectors</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">-2</span>], [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>]])

<span class="cm"># Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors</span>
<span class="va">eigenvalues</span>, <span class="va">eigenvectors</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">eig</span>(<span class="va">A</span>)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Eigenvalues:"</span>, <span class="va">eigenvalues</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Eigenvectors (as columns):"</span>, <span class="va">eigenvectors</span>)

<span class="cm"># Verify: A @ eigenvector = eigenvalue * eigenvector</span>
<span class="va">v1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">eigenvectors</span>[:, <span class="nm">0</span>]  <span class="cm"># First eigenvector</span>
<span class="va">lambda1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">eigenvalues</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]      <span class="cm"># First eigenvalue</span>

<span class="va">lhs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">v1</span>
<span class="va">rhs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">lambda1</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="va">v1</span>

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"A @ v1: {lhs}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"λ1 * v1: {rhs}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Equal (within numerical precision): {np.allclose(lhs, rhs)}"</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Norms measure vector magnitude; different norms for different purposes</li>
      <li>Eigenvalues show scaling along special directions (eigenvectors)</li>
      <li>Eigenvalue decomposition reveals matrix structure</li>
      <li>Largest eigenvalue determines matrix growth properties</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

